\f0\fs24 \cf0 # ANS THROW Codes \'97 64Forth Exception Model\
\
This document describes how **64Forth** implements the ANS Forth 2012 Exception word set (`CATCH` / `THROW` / `ABORT` / `ABORT"`) and how that relates to the standard throw-code table (\'a79.3.1).\
\
It is **not** the old TZForth `kernelThrow` / `errorFlag` migration plan. 64Forth is a hybrid **ARM64 ITC kernel** (`forth.s`) + **Swift host**; exceptions live in the kernel, not in a Swift `errorFlag` path.\
\
**Reference:** [ANS THROW](https://forth-standard.org/standard/exception/THROW), Forth-2012 \'a79.3.1\'96\'a79.3.5.\
\
**Last update:** 2026-07-29 (64Forth v0.8.0).\
\
---\
\
## Model overview\
\
| Mechanism | Where | CATCH-able? | Typical recovery |\
|-----------|--------|-------------|------------------|\
| **`CATCH` / `THROW`** | Kernel CODE (`XCATCH` / `XTHROW`) | **Yes** when a frame is active | Restore IP/DSP/TOS from frame; leave code on data stack |\
| **Uncaught `THROW`** | Kernel `_throw_abort` | N/A | Print `uncaught THROW n`, clear stacks / CATCH nesting, abandon rest of SOURCE |\
| **Soft system faults** | Kernel messages + `_error_abandon` | **Only if** the site uses `THROW` (see table below) | Print message (e.g. `undefined: name`, `stack underflow`), pin `>IN` to end of SOURCE, continue outer interpret |\
| **Host file I/O** | Swift `FileAccess` | Usually **ior** on stack (ANS File-Access), not THROW | Caller checks `ior` |\
\
### `CATCH` frame (implementation)\
\
Return-stack frame (top first): **saved IP**, **saved DSP**, **saved TOS**, **previous handler**. Handler pointer lives in `throw_handler`.\
\
- Success: `(CATCH-OK)` restores the frame and pushes **0**.\
- `0 THROW` is a no-op (drops 0).\
- Nonzero `THROW` restores the frame and leaves the throw code as TOS.\
\
---\
\
## Standard throw codes (ANS \'a79.3.1)\
\
| Code | Condition |\
|------|-----------|\
| -1 | `ABORT` |\
| -2 | `ABORT"` |\
| -3 | Stack underflow |\
| -4 | Stack overflow |\
| -5 | Return stack underflow |\
| -6 | Return stack overflow |\
| -7 | Invalid memory address |\
| -8 | Double-cell number expected |\
| -9 | Division by zero |\
| -10 | Attempt to use zero-length string as a name |\
| -11 | Attempt to use zero-length buffer as a name |\
| -12 | Address not aligned |\
| -13 | Undefined word |\
| -14 | Interpreting a compile-only word |\
| -15 | Uncompleted IF / DO / CASE |\
| -16 | Attempt to use non-existent execution token |\
| -17 | Nesting limit exceeded |\
| -18\'9619 | *(reserved by standard)* |\
| -20\'9621 | Search-order / related (optional) |\
| -22\'9639 | *(reserved by standard)* |\
| -40 | First of **user** throw range |\
| -60\'9665 | Floating-point word set |\
| -66\'9675 | File-access / block word sets |\
\
User applications may `THROW` any nonzero value; codes \uc0\u8804  -40 are conventionally for application-defined faults.\
\
---\
\
## What 64Forth maps today\
\
### Explicit `THROW` sites (CATCH-able)\
\
| Site | Code | Notes |\
|------|------|-------|\
| **`ABORT`** | **-1** | High-level: `-1 THROW` |\
| **`ABORT"`** | **-2** | Nonzero flag: type message, then `-2 THROW` (no message on caught path after TYPE if you THROW before TYPE \'97 we TYPE then THROW; uncaught still soft-aborts) |\
| **Undefined word** (text interpreter, `'`, `[']` interpret) | **-13** | If `throw_handler` nonzero \uc0\u8594  `THROW -13`; else print `undefined: name` and abandon SOURCE |\
| **`?COMP`** (compile-only in interpret) | **-14** | High-level: type `compile only`, then `-14 THROW` |\
| **Application `THROW n`** | *n* | Any nonzero *n* |\
\
### Soft faults (message + abandon; not standard THROW unless noted)\
\
These clear compile state and pin `>IN` to end of the current SOURCE (`_error_abandon`). They do **not** walk a `CATCH` frame unless the site was changed to `THROW`.\
\
| Site | Uncaught behaviour | Future CATCH mapping |\
|------|--------------------|----------------------|\
| Data stack under/overflow (`_check_stack`) | Print `stack underflow` / `stack overflow`, reset DSP | -3 / -4 |\
| Memory fault (SIGSEGV/SIGBUS recovery) | Print `memory access error`, reset stacks | -7 |\
| `ALLOT` past dictionary limits | Print allot error, abandon | -8-ish / implementation |\
| `GROWMEMORYMB` rule violations | Print `? GROWMEMORYMB\'85`, abandon | -20 (implementation) |\
| Dictionary full on compile | Print `dictionary full`, abandon | -8 / -17 |\
| `INCLUDE`/`FLOAD` open failure | Print `can't open: path`, return (nested load does not kill parent SOURCE) | -74 if raised via THROW |\
| File words (`OPEN-FILE`, \'85) | **`ior` on stack** (0 = ok) | Keep ANS ior model |\
\
### Division by zero\
\
`/` and related CODE use ARM `sdiv` with no pre-check. Hardware/undefined behaviour is **not** converted to `-9 THROW` today. Prefer application checks or a future trap mapping.\
\
### Floating-point\
\
FP stack under/overflow is handled in the Swift host (`FloatHost`) and is **not** currently mapped to ANS float codes -60\'96-65. No automatic `THROW` from FP ops.\
\
---\
\
## Uncaught vs caught UX\
\
| Situation | User sees |\
|-----------|-----------|\
| `CATCH` absorbs `THROW` | Only the numeric code on the stack; **no** standard message |\
| Uncaught `THROW n` | `uncaught THROW` + absolute value of *n*, then rest of line abandoned |\
| Uncaught undefined (no CATCH) | `undefined: name`, rest of SOURCE abandoned |\
| Uncaught stack under/overflow | `stack underflow` / `stack overflow`, DSP reset, SOURCE abandoned |\
\
**REPL tip:** wrap risky interpretation:\
\
```forth\
: try  ( c-addr u -- n )  ['] EVALUATE CATCH ;\
S" no-such-word" try .     \\ -13 when CATCH path is used\
```\
\
Typing a missing word alone at the prompt is **uncaught** \uc0\u8594  `undefined: \'85` (not a silent fail).\
\
